  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contract obligations to Arkansas totaled $771M in FY2026 to date. Award activity reached 13,915 awards with an average award size of $55.4K. Obligations fell 39.0% year over year. The current-year totals are partial and represent fiscal year to date activity. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer in Arkansas, obligating $452M so far. The Department of Veterans Affairs follows with $94.6M and the General Services Administration follows with $56.4M. BL HARBERT INTERNATIONAL LLC is the largest vendor by obligations at $102M, followed by ALBERICI CONSTRUCTORS INC at $68.2M and ARMTEC COUNTERMEASURES CO at $47.5M. Commercial and Institutional Building Construction is the largest industry by obligations at $216M, followed by Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction at $121M. Fiscal-year comparisons show higher totals in earlier years, including $1.9B in FY24 and $1.5B in FY21. Agencies and vendors are concentrated in construction, engineering, facilities, and a mix of defense and support services, reflecting the mix of major buyers and large vendor awards in the state.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies drive federal spending in Arkansas?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the top buyer, obligating $452M in FY2026 to date. The Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $94.6M and the General Services Administration obligated $56.4M. Other active buyers include the Department of Justice at $33.5M and the Department of State at $32.3M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who captures the largest federal awards in Arkansas?",
        "body": "BL HARBERT INTERNATIONAL LLC leads vendors with $102M in obligations. ALBERICI CONSTRUCTORS INC obligated $68.2M and ARMTEC COUNTERMEASURES CO obligated $47.5M. Major obligations concentrate in construction-related firms and specialist defense and services contractors."
      }
    ]
